WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.018 --> 00:00:01.560
(audience applauds)

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.290
- This will not be my campaign.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.418
This will be our campaign, all together.

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:08.760
(crowd cheers)

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.170
- [Narrator] Former President Donald Trump

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.580
has announced his plan to run

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.830
in the 2024 presidential election,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.170
after spending months on
the midterm campaign trail

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.840
endorsing a host of Republican candidates.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.690
- Well, I think if they win,
I should get all the credit,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.360
and if they lose, I should
not be blamed at all.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.350
- [Narrator] While Trump remains

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.540
a clear leader in the Republican Party,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.980
his appeal has changed since
he ran in 2016 and 2020.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.010
- There's sort of a fatigue
among some Republicans

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.620
over Donald Trump and his antics

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.810
and what he's left the party with.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:40.920
- [Narrator] So what has changed

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.140
since his first two
presidential campaigns?

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.370
And how could those changes

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.620
impact his political power and influence

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.693
in the 2024 presidential election?

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.110
Several of the Republican
midterm candidates

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.090
that Trump endorsed and campaigned with

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.790
were expected to be a part
of a so-called red wave

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.490
that would carry the party to
complete control of Congress.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.990
- Just three days from now,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.690
the people of Pennsylvania
are going to elect

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.885
Dr. Mehmet Oz to the United States Senate.

00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:07.800
(crowd cheers)

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.240
- [Narrator] But the
outcome of the midterms

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.283
was weaker than expected for the GOP.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.700
Republicans are poised to
flip control of the House,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.290
but several Trump-backed candidates

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.150
in pivotal Senate races struggled,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.000
and the party was unable to
flip control of the Senate.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.230
Candidates who embraced

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.270
Trump's unfounded election fraud claims

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.090
performed particularly
poorly in the midterms.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.280
Blake Masters, who lost in Arizona

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.410
to the Democratic incumbent, Mark Kelly,

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.790
aligned himself with Trump,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.830
supporting national abortion restrictions

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.110
and claims of 2020 election fraud.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.580
He lost independent voters

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.180
by more than 30 percentage
points, according to AP VoteCast.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.890
That, and similar situations

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.390
with other Trump-backed candidates,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.790
like Tudor Dixon and Doug Mastriano,

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.640
called into question the
power of a Trump endorsement

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.780
and whether his brand

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.300
has turned away some independent voters.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.720
- There are people who normally
would not talk on the record

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.000
talking on the record to
reporters and elsewhere

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.400
about, "Donald Trump needs to move on.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.137
It's time for someone else."

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.680
- [Narrator] But one
Trump-backed candidate

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.050
did perform well among voters.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.430
- We've accomplished more

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.610
than anybody thought
possible four years ago.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.980
But we've got so much more to do,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.160
and I have only begun to fight.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.810
- Ron DeSantis's midterm
election victory was so huge

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.300
that it has sort of rewritten

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.180
how Republicans are looking
at the presidential race.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.180
Suddenly, it's not just Donald
Trump's nomination to lose.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.680
- [Narrator] DeSantis has not
announced a presidential bid,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.450
but Trump has already started attacking

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.710
the Florida governor.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.630
- Trump at 71,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.992
Ron DeSanctimonious at 10%.

00:02:45.992 --> 00:02:48.690
- [Narrator] DeSantis's victory
comes after some Republicans

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.010
have openly called for the party

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.520
to move past the former president
and look for a new leader.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.560
- It's basically the
third election in a row

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.500
that Donald Trump has cost us the race.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.450
And it's like, you know,
three strikes, you're out.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.050
- There is a growing sense of Republicans

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.050
identifying more as members of the GOP,

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.120
versus supporters of Donald Trump.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.740
- [Narrator] Following
the midterm elections,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.090
some Republicans have said

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.430
that Trump's influence
and endorsement choices

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.770
have cost the party winnable races.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.510
- Those who are most closely aligned

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.748
with the former president underperformed.

00:03:21.748 --> 00:03:23.640
Those who are talking about the future,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.790
who had managed their states
well, they overperformed.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.170
- [Narrator] Other potential challengers

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.580
for the Republican nomination

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.770
include Trump's 2016 running mate,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.780
former Vice President Mike Pence,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.070
who parted ways with Trump

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.503
following the January 6th
attack on the Capitol.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.260
- One thing that will be
really interesting to watch

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.540
is how not just voters but the donors,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.660
the big money people, and who
they put their money behind.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.000
A lot of them do want to see someone else.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.650
- [Narrator] Trump has
a proven track record

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.260
when it comes to online
and grassroots fundraising,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.840
even after leaving the Oval Office.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.600
Earlier this year, a Federal
Election Commission filing

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.230
showed that Trump had more
than $120 million in cash

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.870
across his post-presidential
political operation.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.180
But with the presence of
other potential front-runners

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.910
in the presidential
race, like Ron DeSantis,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.060
it remains to be seen where
donors will invest their money.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.440
- They are looking to someone else

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.520
that can kind of bring the party

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.590
a little bit back to where it had been,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.600
a more traditional kind of conservatism.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.890
- [Narrator] Trump also moves forward

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.630
amid a host of legal issues,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.850
including civil and
criminal investigations

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.250
into his company, Trump Organization,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.780
the January 6th probe,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.310
and a federal investigation

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.587
into mishandled classified
White House records.

00:04:35.587 --> 00:04:38.310
- [Crowd Chanting] Trump!
Trump! Trump! Trump!

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.720
- [Narrator] Trump still remains popular

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.510
with right-leaning voters
and allies within the party,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.310
but he now faces new challenges

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.870
that could influence his candidacy

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.730
ahead of the presidential election.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.960
- He still is the number one
beloved figure in the party

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.820
among the voters, the base there.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.110
Now there are cracks in that,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.690
but Donald Trump is
still a very, very strong

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.550
and powerful force in the party.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.980
And that should not be overlooked,

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.930
even as there are some cracks

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.061
and rising stars like Ron DeSantis.

00:05:07.061 --> 00:05:10.228
(contemplative music)

